---
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import Seo from '../components/Seo.astro';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { breadcrumbSchema, graph, type Crumb } from '../lib/schema.ts';
import { SITE } from '../lib/site.ts';

const crumbs: Crumb[] = [
	{ name: 'Home', href: '/' },
	{ name: 'About', href: '/about' },
];
---

<BaseLayout>
	<Seo
		slot="head"
		title="About"
		description="How the hairstyle previews are made, why they are illustrations rather than photographs, and what this site does and does not do."
		path="/about"
	>
		<script type="application/ld+json" is:inline set:html={graph(breadcrumbSchema(crumbs))} />
	</Seo>

	<div class="container-page pt-6"><Breadcrumbs crumbs={crumbs} /></div>

	<article class="container-prose py-10 pb-16">
		<h1 class="text-4xl leading-[1.05] sm:text-5xl">About {SITE.name}</h1>
		<div class="editorial mt-8">
			<p>
				This is a hairstyle catalogue with a preview tool attached. You can browse 45 cuts,
				read what each one actually demands of you, and then put any of them on a photo of
				your own face to see roughly how the shape sits.
			</p>

			<h2>How the previews work</h2>
			<p>
				Every hairstyle here is a drawn asset, built from a parametric model of a head. Each
				one records where a person's eyes sit relative to the hair. When you click your own
				two eyes on your photo, the browser solves the transform that maps those two points
				onto yours — which fixes the scale, the rotation and the position in one step — and
				composites the hair onto the image.
			</p>
			<p>
				That is all it is: geometry and image compositing, running in your browser. There is
				no machine learning, no image generation, and no server involved in producing the
				result.
			</p>

			<h2>What the previews are not</h2>
			<p>
				They are <strong>illustrations, not photographs</strong>. The hair is drawn rather
				than photographed, so a preview shows you the silhouette, the volume and roughly
				where the hairline will sit — not what your actual hair will look like in that cut.
			</p>
			<p>
				That distinction matters. Use a preview to rule shapes in and out and to see how a
				fade height or a fringe length changes your proportions. Do not use it as a
				photorealistic prediction, because it is not one and nothing that runs entirely in a
				browser without a model behind it could be.
			</p>

			<h2>Your photo</h2>
			<p>
				Your photo never leaves your device. It is read directly into a canvas element and
				discarded when you close the tab. There is no upload, no account, and no analytics
				attached to it. You can verify this yourself by opening your browser's network panel
				while you use the studio — there will be no outbound request carrying your image.
			</p>
			<p>
				More detail is on the <a href="/privacy">privacy page</a>.
			</p>

			<h2>The written guidance</h2>
			<p>
				The advice on each hairstyle page is written to be useful rather than encouraging.
				Where a cut demands a fortnightly barber visit, it says so. Where a face shape is a
				poor match, it says that too, and suggests the closer alternative. A catalogue that
				tells you everything suits you is not a catalogue, it is a shop window.
			</p>
		</div>

		<div class="mt-12 rounded-card border border-copper-200 bg-copper-50 p-6 text-center sm:p-8">
			<h2 class="font-display text-2xl">Try it yourself</h2>
			<p class="mx-auto mt-2 max-w-md text-sm leading-relaxed text-stone-600">
				A photo, two clicks, and 45 hairstyles to move through.
			</p>
			<a
				href="/try"
				class="mt-5 inline-flex rounded-full bg-ink px-6 py-3 text-sm font-medium text-paper transition-colors hover:bg-copper-600"
			>
				Open the studio
			</a>
		</div>
	</article>
</BaseLayout>
